    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67004,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chondromas usually occur in the peripheral lung; rarely they may be endobronchial.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:06:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67005,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients are usually asymptomatic, but reported symptoms include cough, chest pain, dyspnoea, and pneumonia {18043038}. Chondromas occur in all pulmonary lobes; they tend to be multiple (averaging three) in Carney triad and solitary in sporadic cases {18043038; 31236950}. Imaging shows well-delineated nodules that may be calcified, most commonly with a popcorn-like or central pattern {18043038}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:06:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67006,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chondromas are typically found in young woman (aged &lt;&nbsp;30&nbsp;years) affected by Carney triad {18043038; 31236950; 19723753}. Rare sporadic examples have male predominance and older average age (mean: 53&nbsp;years) {18043038}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:06:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67007,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "<p>Most affected patients have Carney triad, a non-hereditary tumour syndrome characterized by gastrointestinal stromal tumours, pulmonary chondromas, and paragangliomas {19723753; 19522824}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-18T17:28:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67008,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "<p>Gain of chromosome&nbsp;6 and loss of 1q have been reported in pulmonary chondroma {19522824}. Functional deficiency of succinate dehydrogenase is the underlying mechanism of Carney triad, driven by hypermethylation of <em>SDHC</em> {28739378}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-18T17:28:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67009,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "<p>Chondromas are well circumscribed and lobulated and have a grey-white to bluish gritty cut surface {18043038}. The average size is 28 mm (range: 8&ndash;75 mm) {18043038}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-02T10:58:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67010,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chondromas are circumscribed nodules of pure hyaline cartilage with frequent myxoid change. The cartilage is moderately cellular without atypia and is surrounded by a fibrous pseudocapsule without invagination of the overlying bronchial epithelium. Calcification and ossification are common, and ossification may be accompanied by adipose tissue.</p>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes pulmonary hamartoma and chondrosarcoma. Hamartomas have entrapped respiratory epithelium between the lobules of cartilage, and they frequently contain other mesenchymal elements (fat, smooth muscle, myxoid spindle cells). Primary pulmonary chondrosarcomas are exceptionally rare; chondrosarcomas in the lung are more often metastases from high-grade skeletal tumours, with significantly increased cellularity and atypia {9827715; 21496864}.</p>\r\n<p>Carney-associated chondromas show frequent loss of SDHB by immunohistochemistry, in contrast to hamartomas {31236950}, and this can be a useful diagnostic adjunct when the distinction is difficult.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:06:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67011,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 91,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-11-11T12:06:42"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
